==== step 8/12: lab matrix
[demo] Five rows: deploy a scenario, scan it with the real scanner, announce the forged route, let the CA try to issue to the attacker. The results block follows when the run ends: per row, the scanner's label, who answered each CA perspective (attacker = captured) and the CA's decision against the expectation. Every transcript is in lab/logs/.
$ uv run lab/labctl.py matrix
11:16:06 logs: /home/djamla/hijackscan/lab/logs/20261101T101606Z-matrix (latest-matrix -> 20261101T101606Z-matrix)      

how to read a row ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Each row: deploy one scenario, scan it with the real scanner, announce the forged route, let the CA try to issue to the attacker, withdraw the route, let the owner's account try (the control). The scanner's label must predict the CA's decision; the row's expectation is in its banner.
the lines of evidence
  [request  ]  what is asked: the domain, the ACME account (attacker or legit), the method
  [dns      ]  one lookup through the validating resolver: rcode and AD flag (ad=1 validated, SERVFAIL bogus)
  [caa      ]  the CAA walk victim.rs -> rs -> . (RFC 8659) and what the issue tag allows (RFC 8657)
  [http-01  ]  one perspective: behind which AS it sits and which host answered (attacker = hijacked route)
  [quorum   ]  CA/B MPIC rule: the primary and at least 3 of 4 remotes must corroborate
  [decision ]  ISSUED or REFUSED and the reason; this line is what the matrix checks
  attack ...   control plane (paths at the collector) and data plane (who answers the web and ns addresses)


row: baseline / attack web  (expect scanner open-web, CA -> attacker ISSUED) ───────────────────────────────────────────
  loose ROA (/24, maxLength 28), no CAA, unsigned zone: the forged /28 is RPKI-valid, so every CA perspective except the victim's own AS lands on the attacker, and the attacker gets the certificate.
